problemStatement:
Consider the code snippet:
```c
int main() {
  fork();
  if (fork() == 0) {
    fork();
  }
  printf("PRIME\n");
  return 0;
}
```
How many total processes are created (including parent)? How many times is 'PRIME' printed?

In Peterson's solution for two processes (0 and 1):
```c
flag[i] = true;
turn = j;
while (flag[j] && turn == j);
// critical section
flag[i] = false;
```
Show why reversing the order of statements to `turn = j; flag[i] = true;` violates mutual exclusion.

sampleAnswer:
Fair Readers-Writers using an entry semaphore `service_queue`:
Semaphores: `mutex = 1` (protects read_count), `wrt = 1` (resource lock), `service_queue = 1` (FIFO turnstiles).

Reader:
```c
wait(service_queue);
wait(mutex);
read_count++;
if (read_count == 1) wait(wrt);
signal(mutex);
signal(service_queue); // Reader frees queue immediately

// Reading...

wait(mutex);
read_count--;
if (read_count == 0) signal(wrt);
signal(mutex);
```

Writer:
```c
wait(service_queue); // Writer claims queue; no new readers can pass service_queue!
wait(wrt);
signal(service_queue);

// Writing...

signal(wrt);
```
